 things that were coming out of  there and you know there was one of the  dishes that we serve at the beginning of  Masseria is a tomato sauce that as a kid  I can remember just taking a piece of  bread and swiping the side of the pot to  get that little piece that's drying on  the side so  to mimic that flavor so there's all  these pieces of nostalgia that wonderful  you you open your first restaurant in  2015  correct after working in some pretty big  establishments not too bad you did but  when did you know it was the right time  to make that mark just felt good I think  it was though the end goal was always to  go in open my own space up and it was  just timing I've worked for a lot of  great people had a lot of good mentors  and it was just kind of the natural  progression of leading into taking that  next step one of the great things I love  about chef here is that basically asked  him what which what the latest trends  are in food and he said we don't follow  trends hmm it's not about the trends  it's about it's about classic stuff  classic great stuff and we're taking  things that inspire us and that's the  greatest thing of travel is going to  experience new new techniques new food  whether it's going to Italy or going to  South America or just meeting new people  and you get to see different things that  you can bring into your style of cooking  and kind of do that storytelling it's  such different food in some ways Italian  food Greek food but at the same time  there's got to be something that ties it  together there's a lot of similarities  and then there's a lot of things that  separate them as well so that's going  into telling the other half of my  heritage I think that's the good piece  of it's a new challenge as a cook where  you're learning something new you're  learning new techniques and it's as you  go through the research and go through  and the storytelling and the dishes and  the history of the cuisine and how  things were established and where  they've come from from 200 300 years ago  to where we are today where you know  when you have somebody have one of these  meals whether it's Italian or Greek how  do you want them to walk out what do you  want them to walk away thinking cool I  mean it's the sense of hospitality so  you want people to be taken care of you  want them to be pampered and nurtured  and just kind of feel like they've been  your true guests for the evening as they  leave the restaurant certainly are well  have you sign and ask you if you can  have this meal with anyone past or  present who would it be  I would say my grandparents just because  they've never had the ability to see  where this is went and created to so to  be able to have them come back and see  where we are kind of years later would  be great the influence they started all  thank you chef Nick Stefanelli if you  would like more on him or the dish you  can head to our website at CBS  this morning calm

